HYPOTHESIS

Economics (experimental economics — the same block as replication project `ee`, 18 original/replication pairs) is the domain in which the majority of authors tend to be male for BOTH original and replication studies. Pooling the actual people inside the domain (male authors / all authors, from authors_male x n_authors): originals 42/48 = 87.5% male, replications 48/59 = 81.4% male — both strictly above 0.5 (margins +0.375 and +0.314). All four metrics agree and so does the tie handling: share of pairs with a male author majority 77.8% (14/18) for originals and 72.2% (13/18) for replications, unweighted mean male ratio 0.870 (o) vs 0.837 (r), median 1.00 vs 1.00, and only 4 (o) / 2 (r) of 18 pairs sit exactly at 0.5. Economics is the ONLY discipline that passes >0.5 on both sides under all four metrics in all three unit-of-analysis variants (unit-level n=141, naive 927 rows, unit-level excluding drop==True n=131). The psychology disciplines do not: Social (n=74) has 64.8% male authors in originals but 53.4% in replications, Cognitive (n=49) 70.6% vs 51.9% — replication-side margins of only +0.034 and +0.019 — and both fail as soon as the metric is 'share of pairs with a male-majority team' (48.7% and 44.9%, below 0.5) or the median pair (exactly 0.5 = tie). Their slim replication-side margin comes entirely from the Many Labs consortium teams (ml1: 0.560 male among 51 authors; ml3: 0.516 among 64 authors), each of which is one single team shared by every study of its project; if each consortium team is counted once per project instead of once per study, Social falls to 49.4% and Cognitive to 50.3%, i.e. at or below the threshold, while Economics stays at 81.4%. In the parallel project cut the answer is the identical block, ee (0.875 original vs 0.814 replication, pass on all four metrics), and rpp fails outright (0.649 vs 0.460; male-majority replication teams in only 33.0% of its 97 pairs); ml1 and ml3 do clear 0.5 on all four metrics in that cut as well, but that is a degenerate pass — their entire replication side is one consortium with a 0.560/0.516 margin, one distinct value, an effective sample of one team, not 16 and 10 independent teams. So discipline and project converge on Economics/ee as the domain where authorship is male-majority on both sides, and the ambiguity of the word 'domain' does not change that answer. Main caveat: original and replication author sets are two different groups of people (a replication team is not the original cohort), and for Many Labs 1/3 the replication side is a single 51-/64-author consortium rather than per-study teams.

WORKFLOW

Descriptive analysis only — no models, no significance tests, no use of replicated/relative_es. (1) Inspection: file1 = 927 rows x 57 cols, 141 unique O/R ids (135 distinct original studies), 115 ids with 1 row, 16 with 37 rows (ml1) and 10 with 22 rows (ml3); authors_male.o/.r are float in [0,1] with 0 missing values, 312 (o) and 75 (r) values exactly 0.5; there is no `authors.r` column; ee == Economics 1:1 (18/18). (2) File 2 (meta-regression_replication_success_data..., 143x15) is NOT used: it carries no author-gender column and has no join key to file1 (study vs title / authors.o overlap = 0). (3) Metric per domain per side: PRIMARY pooled author-level male share = SUM(authors_male*n_authors)/SUM(n_authors); plus share of pairs with ratio>0.5, unweighted mean and median of pair-level ratios, and the count of 0.5 ties. Threshold strictly >0.5; a ratio of exactly 0.5 is a tie, not a majority. (4) Three unit variants reported side by side: (a) one row per id, aggregated row used for ml1/ml3 [primary, n=141]; (b) all 927 rows [NAIVE / CONTAMINATED — the pooled ml1/ml3 replication ratio is copied into 37/22 lab rows, and 80 ml1 lab rows carry 0.56 with n_authors.r=51 instead of the lab's own authors, so every domain is inflated toward the consortium value]; (c) unit-level with drop==True rows removed [sensitivity, n=131]. (5) Both taxonomies reported (discipline = headline per the research question, project = parallel) plus the discipline x project crosstab and a metric x domain pass matrix; consortium-concentration diagnostics (number of distinct replication ratios per domain) included. (6) Headline numbers independently re-derived from integer author counts (42/48 and 48/59 for Economics). (7) Gender comes only from the supplied ratio columns; no inference of gender from author names.
